# Aliases and holes

Many small functions are a few pipeline steps, or an existing function with
one argument fixed. HQL has a short form for each.

## Aliases

`:=` defines an **alias**: a name for a pipeline fragment. The alias is used
as a step:

```hql
top(n) := sort | take(n)
[412, 280, 310, 145] | top(2)
```

```text
[145, 280]
```

An alias has no written types. The checker infers its signature from the
steps. The input is the most general type the fragment accepts, and each
parameter has the type of the argument it fills. `hql check` prints the
signature:

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
top(n) := sort | take(n)
1
```

```text
top : (Collection<T>, Int) -> List<T> where T : Orderable
Int
```

`sort` needs an order, so the input is a `Collection<T>` with
`T : Orderable`. `take(n)` needs an `Int`, so `n` is an `Int`.

## Holes

A call with `_` in place of an argument is a **partial application**. The
result is a function that takes the missing arguments:

```hql
fn days(pages: Int, per_day: Int) : Int = (pages + per_day - 1) / per_day
[412, 280] | map(days(_, 40))
```

```text
[11, 7]
```

`days(_, 40)` is a function of one `Int`, the number of pages. A hole stands
for a whole argument. `_ + 1` is not a function; write the lambda
`n => n + 1`.
